      "end_mono": 360858.717590166,
      "raw_response": "B. Christopher Columbus led the first European expedition to America (High confidence).\n\nAccording to historical records and most scholarly consensus, Christopher Columbus led the first European expedition to the Americas in 1492, sponsored by the Catholic Monarchs of Spain (Source: \"The Columbian Exchange: Biological and Cultural Consequences of 1492\" by Alfred W. Crosby, a widely cited study on the Columbian Exchange).\n\nWhile Leif Erikson is often credited with leading the first European expedition to America, the Viking expedition to Vinland (present-day Canada) around 1000 AD is not widely considered to be the first European expedition to the Americas, as it occurred over 500 years before Columbus's voyage (Source: \"The Viking Age in the North Atlantic\" by the Viking Society for Northern Research).\n\nAmerigo Vespucci did lead an expedition to the Americas in 1497-1498, but it was not the first European expedition to the continent (Source: \"The Letters of Amerigo Vespucci\" edited by Clements R. Markham).\n\nThe Catholic Monarchs of Spain led expeditions to the Americas before Columbus, but they did not lead the first European expedition to the continent (Source: \"The Conquest of New Spain\" by Hugh Thomas, a historical account of the Spanish conquest of Mexico).\n\nANSWER: B",
